Hydrocephalus
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Rapidly increasing head circumference, Bulging anterior fontanelle, Sunsetting eyes, Ventricular dilation on imaging

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-026
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of extreme irritability, vomiting, and suspected raised intracranial pressure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Infant and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 5-month-old infant with a known abnormally rapid increase in head circumference, now presenting with sudden-onset projectile vomiting, a tense fontanelle, and continuous high-pitched crying for the past 4 hours. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the imminent, life-threatening risk of cerebral herniation and irreversible brain damage secondary to acute decompensated hydrocephalus, necessitating urgent clinical evaluation to expedite emergency neurosurgical transfer.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He has been screaming a horrific, high-pitched cry all afternoon, throwing his milk forcefully across the room, and his soft spot looks huge.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 4-hour history of acute deterioration in an infant currently under investigation for an abnormally rapid increase in head circumference. The infant developed extreme irritability and a continuous high-pitched cry that is entirely uncharacteristic. Over the past 2 hours, this progressed to three episodes of forceful, non-bilious projectile vomiting and complete refusal of feeds. The parents noted a visibly bulging and tense anterior fontanelle that remains rigid even when the infant briefly pauses crying. The infant has recently developed progressive lethargy between crying spells. A known history of widely separated cranial sutures with prominent scalp veins is documented.
Functional Status: Fully dependent infant; currently demonstrating an altered level of consciousness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of death from brainstem herniation; extreme parental panic.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/60 mmHg (Elevated for age) | HR: 90 bpm (Relative bradycardia) | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 22 bpm (Irregular) | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 6.8 Kg
General: Infant is pale, alternating between severe high-pitched irritability and profound lethargy.
Physical Exam: Head circumference is tracked >99th percentile. Cranial exam confirms a markedly bulging anterior fontanelle that is tense to palpation, alongside widely separated cranial sutures and engorged scalp veins. Neurological exam reveals a striking upward gaze palsy ('sunsetting' of the eyes) and sluggish, unequal pupillary responses. Motor exam demonstrates emerging spasticity and increased muscle tone in the lower extremities. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable aside from Cushing's triad elements (hypertension, relative bradycardia, irregular respirations). The diagnostic tetrad is clinically complete (rapidly increasing head circumference, bulging anterior fontanelle, sunsetting eyes), strongly suggesting impending ventricular dilation on imaging.
Environmental Observations: Vomitus present on bedding; the house is otherwise appropriately maintained.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications; paracetamol was given earlier without any effect on the continuous crying.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hydrocephalus (with acute severe raised intracranial pressure) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause due to the absence of fever and the specific mechanical signs of macrocephaly, though it remains a secondary risk or differential requiring ED workup.

Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a tense fontanelle, sunsetting eyes, and the forceful, non-nausea-driven nature of the projectile vomiting (central vomiting).

Space-Occupying Lesion (Tumor) - Ruled out/unlikely immediately clinically, but presents identical signs of raised ICP; definitive diagnosis relies on emergent imaging.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Neurosurgical emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic, critical signs of acutely raised intracranial pressure: projectile vomiting, a tense fontanelle, sunsetting eyes, and a Cushing's response (hypertension and bradycardia). These objective findings confirmed a catastrophic failure of cerebrospinal fluid dynamics, leading to severe brain compression. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because untreated acute hydrocephalus leads rapidly to fatal brainstem herniation. Immediate, emergency transfer to a pediatric neurosurgical center was mandatory for urgent imaging (CT/MRI to confirm ventricular dilation) and likely emergent ventricular drain placement.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Positioned the infant with the head of the bed elevated strictly to 30 degrees to optimize cerebral venous drainage and lower intracranial pressure. Kept the infant strictly nil by mouth to prevent aspiration during transport and prepare for potential emergency surgery. Activated 000 for immediate emergency ambulance transport to the Paediatric Emergency Department.
Positive Behavioral Change: N/A - Acute life-threatening emergency requiring immediate transfer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedic crew and receiving pediatric neurosurgical registrar.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently being transported to the Emergency Department via ambulance under my direct supervision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying cause of the fluid buildup requires specialized imaging at the hospital; the immediate goal is purely to relieve the pressure on the brain.
Clinical Course: The infant will require intensive care admission and likely emergency neurosurgery (e.g., placing a shunt or drain) to permanently divert the excess fluid and save brain function.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not lay the infant completely flat at any time. Do not attempt to give any fluids by mouth. Follow all instructions from the emergency medical team implicitly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fluid that normally cushions the brain is trapped and building up rapidly, putting massive pressure on the brain tissue. This pressure is causing the vomiting, the bulging soft spot, and the eye changes. Elevating his head helps drain some blood away from the brain to slightly lower the pressure, but he needs emergency surgery to drain the fluid.
Actionable Steps: Pack a bag for a prolonged hospital stay. Keep his head elevated. Follow the paramedics exactly.

